 ],
 "literal": "In the forest-cultivator's field, like gold, the small millet / having been cut and eaten by parrots, leaving cropped stubble, / because of the presence of heavy rain, sprouts leaves; like that, my / strength even though died, I am indeed alive, O friend, in my / loneliness after he newly consumed my beauty.",
 "translation": "O friend! In the forest-cultivator's field, the gold-like small millet is cut and eaten by parrots, leaving behind cropped stubble. Yet, when heavy rains fall, that stubble sprouts leaves again. Just like that, even though my strength has died, I still remain alive in this loneliness, after he newly consumed my beauty.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! தினைப்புனக் காவலாளியின் வயலில், பொன்னைப் போன்ற சிறு தினையைக் கிளிகள் கொத்தித் தின்ற பின் எஞ்சிய மொட்டையான தினைத்தாள்கள், பெருமழை பெய்யும்போது மீண்டும் தழைத்து இலைவிடுவது போல, என் அழகைப் புதிதாக நுகர்ந்துவிட்டு அவர் பிரிந்ததால் ஏற்பட்ட இந்தத் தனிமையிலும், என் மனவலிமை அழிந்துபோன பிறகும் நான் இன்னும் உயிரோடு இருக்கிறேன்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante in this Kuriñci (mountainous) landscape poem. The hero has departed after enjoying union with her, leaving her in deep grief, and the confidante is worried about her survival. The heroine compares her state to the cropped stubble of millet in a forest field. Parrots have eaten the golden millet, leaving only bare, cropped stalks, but when heavy rains fall, these seemingly dead stalks sprout fresh green leaves. The parrots eating the millet represents the hero enjoying the heroine's beauty, and the bare stubble represents her depleted state after his departure. The heavy rain represents the hope of the hero's return, which keeps her alive despite her lost strength.",
